              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#on_minkowski_spacetime"} On Minkowski spacetime
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesOnCurvedSpacetimes"} On curved spacetimes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#local_covariance"} Local covariance
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %em causal perturbation theory
            is a mathematically rigorous construction of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"}> gauge
            \-)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            by Stückelberg-Bogoliubov-Epstein-Glaser (
            %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"}> Epstein-Glaser 73
            ).
          %p
            Causal perturbation theory may be regarded as providing a well-defined consistent generalization from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            of the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dyson+formula"} Dyson formula
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"}> time-ordered products
            ”) in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction+picture"} interaction picture
            \.
          %p
            A key idea of causal perturbation theory is that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is considered multiplied with some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            which has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatic switching
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            would naively be given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dyson+formula"} Dyson formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi g
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi T
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi X
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi dvol
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_g \coloneqq T \exp\left( \int_X g(x) V(x) dvol(x) \right)
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term, and “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            ” indicating the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatizing
            the key structural properties of this “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"}> adiabatically switched
            ”
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+additivity"} causal additivity
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal ordering
            (whence the name of the approach) and then proving by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            that solutions to these axioms exist.
          %p
            It turns out that at each step in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            (corresponding to each loop order) a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
            has to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "extension%20of%20distributions#SolutionSpaceOfPointExtensions"} extended to a point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            takes place. A key theorem (
            %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"}> Epstein-Glaser 73, section 5
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "extension+of+distributions#SpaceOfPointExtensions"} this prop.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-supported+distributions"} point-supported distributions
            at that point. One may organize the distributions that need to be extended into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            (
            %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagenKellerRejzner14"}> Dütsch-Fredenhagen-Keller-Rejzner 14
            ). This way the freedom in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            is identified with the traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            freedom in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization
            for more on this point.
          %p
            (It may be argued, vividly so in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Scharf95"} Scharf 95, p. 181-182
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
          %p
            In fact the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra"} interacting field algebra
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            constructed via causal perturbation theory this way is a model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov%27s+formal+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov's formal deformation quantization
            of the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
            (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#HawkinsRejzner16"} Hawkins-Rejzner 16
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
          %p
            The key axiom imposed on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            in causal perturbation theory is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
            separated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supports"} supports
            then the S-matrix
            %em factors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo +
                      %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi h
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{g + h} = S_g S_h
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(..)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            are handled by splitting of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            using tools from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+divergences"} ultraviolet divergences
          %p
            Originally the idea was that in the end the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \to 1
            had to be taken, removing the adiabatic switching of the the compact support of the interaction. In general this limit does not exist (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergences"}> infrared divergences
            ”, e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#AAS10"} AAS 10, section 6
          %p
            But in (
            %a{:href => "#IlinSlavnov78"}> Il’in-Slavnov 78
            ) it was observed that in fact the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            on any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+subsets"} bounded
            region may be computed with a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
            contains the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+closure"} causal closure
            of that region. Later in (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"}> Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            ) it was pointed out that this means that causal perturbation theory in fact serves to construct the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+local+net+of+observables"} causally local net of observables
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            (see
            %a{:href => "S-matrix#PerturbativeQuantumObservablesIsLocalnet"} this prop
            for details), as in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            and in fact as in the axioms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebras"} formal power series algebras
            (as befits a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            (suitable for
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            (This takes care of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"}> algebraic adiabatic limit
            ”, which defines the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+adiabatic+limit"} weak adiabatic limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
          %p
            This way causal perturbation theory leads to a unification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#MainTheoremOfRenormalization Main theorem of renormalization
          %p
            A central result in causal perturbation theory is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization+theory"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em renormalization schemes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \mapsto S(V)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            terms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \colon V \to V'
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S' = S \circ Z
                  \\,.
          %p
            The collection of these operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            forms a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/St%C3%BCckelberg-Petermann+renormalization+group"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
          %p
            This is a mathematical reflection of the idea that renormalization is about regarding a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            at some energy scale and then discovering that at higher energy there is a more fundamental interaction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(V)
            which effectively looks like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            at lower energy.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
          %h2#References References
          %p Lecture notes in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} geometry of physics – perturbative quantum field theory
          %p The method is due to
          %ul
            %li#EpsteinGlaser73
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Epstein"}> Henri Epstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Glaser"} Vladimir Glaser
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Role+of+locality+in+perturbation+theory"} The Role of locality in perturbation theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=AIHPA_1973__19_3_211_0"} Numdam
          %p with precursors in
          %ul
            %li#Stueckelberg49
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
                , D. Rivier, Helv. Phys. Acta, 22 (1949) 215.
            %li#Stueckelberg51
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
                , J. Green, Helv. Phys. Acta, 24 (1951) 153.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
                , A. Peterman, ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em La normalisation des constants dans la theorie des quanta
            %li#BogoliubovShirkov59
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolay+Bogoliubov"}> Nikolay Bogoliubov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Shirkov"} Dmitry Shirkov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to the Theory of Quantized Fields
          %p
            whence sometimes called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em Stückelberg-Bogoliubov-Epstein-Glaser method
          %p
            The expression of causal perturbation theory in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            techniques is due to
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagenKellerRejzner14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
              = succeed "," do
                %em Dimensional Regularization in Position Space, and a Forest Formula for Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
              %p
                55(12), 122303 (2014) (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.5424"}> arXiv:1311.5424
                )
          %p
            That causal perturbation theory (in the generality of curved spacetimes, see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesOnCurvedSpacetimes"} below
            = succeed "-)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the interacting Lagrangian density was shown for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#Collini16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Collini"}> Giovanni Collini
              ,
              %em Fedosov Quantization and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09626"}> arXiv:1603.09626
              )
          %p and for the interacting scalar field in the toy example of regular non-local interactions in
          %ul
            %li#HawkinsRejzner16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eli+Hawkins"}> Eli Hawkins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
              %em The Star Product in Interacting Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.09157"}> arXiv:1612.09157
              )
          %h3#on_minkowski_spacetime On Minkowski spacetime
          %p
            Causal perturbation theory has been worked out in detail for the example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            in
          %ul
            %li#Scharf95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Finite Quantum Electrodynamics – The Causal Approach
          %p
            and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"} perturbative quantum gravity
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            in
          %ul
            %li#Scharf01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Gauge+Theories+--+A+True+Ghost+Story"} Quantum Gauge Theories – A True Ghost Story
          %p Non-technical exposition of this includes
          %ul
            %li#Neumaier15
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arnold+Neumaier"}> Arnold Neumaier
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/causal-perturbation-theory/"} Causal Perturbation Theory
                (2015)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arnold+Neumaier"}> Arnold Neumaier
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~neum/physfaq/topics/causalQFT.html"} Action-based quantum field theory and causal perturbation theory
                (one section in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~neum/physfaq/physics-faq.html"} A theoretical physics FAQ
          %p Technical review includes
          %ul
            %li#AAS10
              %p
                Andreas Aste, Cyrill von Arx,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"} Günter Scharf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Regularization in quantum field theory from the causal point of view
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.1952"} arXiv:0906.1952
            %li
              %p
                Christian Pöselt,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The method of Epstein and Glaser I
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://wwwthep.physik.uni-mainz.de/~scheck/Poeselt.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#ReferencesOnCurvedSpacetimes On curved spacetimes
          %p
            The generalization of causal perturbation theory to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetimes"} quantum field theory on curved spacetimes
            is developed in
          %ul
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"} math-ph/9903028
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Local Wick polynomials and time ordered products of quantum fields in curved spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0103074"} arXiv:gr-qc/0103074
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Existence of local covariant time ordered products of quantum fields in curved spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0111108"} arXiv:gr-qc/0111108
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the renormalization group in curved spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0209029"} arXiv:gr-qc/0209029
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Conservation of the stress tensor in perturbative interacting quantum field theory in curved spacetimes
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0404074"} arXi:gr-qc/0404074
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2038"} arXiv:0901.2038
          %p Review is in
          %ul
            %li#HollandsWald14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum fields in curved spacetime
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2026"} arXiv:1401.2026
          %h3#local_covariance Local covariance
          %p
            The observation that the method of causal perturbation theory naturally leads to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#IlinSlavnov78
              V. A. Il’in and D. S. Slavnov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Observable algebras in the S-matrix approach
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/135575"} spire
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01035870"} doi
          %p and was re-discovered and then popularized in
          %ul
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen00
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"} math-ph/9903028
          %p Review includes
          %ul
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
              %em Perturbative Construction of Models of Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.07814"}> arXiv:1503.07814
              )
          %p
            For more on this see the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
        .revisedby
